Testing the code that receives webhooks without waiting for real events - fixture deliveries built and signed in tests, testing every rejection path, replaying recorded deliveries, the service's own test events and CLI tools, and exposing a local endpoint with a tunnel during development. The awkward part Webhook code runs when another system decides to call it, which makes it awkward to test: you cannot easily make a real payment fail at three in the morning. The answer is to build deliveries yourself. Fixture deliveries A test helper that builds a signed request from an event, exactly as the service would, lets you test the whole receiver: python-norun def make_delivery(secret, event, timestamp, path="/webhooks"): body = json.dumps(event, separators=(",", ":"), sort_keys=True).encode() signature = hmac.new(secret, f"{timestamp}.".encode() + body, hashlib.sha256).hexdigest() head = (f"POST {path} HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost
Content-Type: application/json
" f"Content-Length: {len(body)}
Webhook-Signature: t={timestamp},v1={signature}

") return head.encode() + body With it, each test is one line of setup and one assertion: - a valid delivery is accepted and processed once ; - the same delivery again is accepted but not processed again; - a delivery signed with the wrong secret , with a changed body , or with an old timestamp is rejected; - a delivery for an unknown event type is accepted and ignored; - events delivered out of order leave the newest state; - a handler that fails gets a 500, and a later retry of the same event succeeds. Practice 4.05 writes this helper; the project's tests use the same idea. Recorded deliveries Services show recent deliveries (payload, headers and your response) in their dashboard, and many let you resend one. Save a few real payloads (with personal data removed) as test fixtures: they catch differences between the documentation's examples and what the service really sends, such as a field that is sometimes null . Test events and tools - Most services have a "send test webhook" button or an API to trigger sample events. - Some ship a CLI that forwards events from their test mode to your local machine. - In test mode , you can create the objects (a test payment with a test card) that trigger real events end to end. Reaching your laptop A service cannot deliver to http://127.0.0.1:8080 . During development, a tunnel service gives your local port a temporary public HTTPS URL; you register that URL as the webhook endpoint in test mode . Remember to remove the subscription afterwards, and never point live-mode webhooks at a laptop. Check yourself quiz ? What does a fixture delivery helper let you test that the service's dashboard cannot? + Every rejection and edge case, repeatably, with no real events - The service's own retry schedule and its timing - Whether the service's servers are up right now - How fast the public internet delivers requests Building signed deliveries yourself means a test can send a wrong secret, a changed body or an old timestamp whenever it likes. ? Which three deliveries should a receiver's tests reject? - A duplicate event, an unknown event type and an out-of-order event - A delivery with sorted keys, one with spaces and one with a long body + One signed with the wrong secret, one with a changed body, and one with a timestamp too old - Any delivery sent from a test helper rather than the real service Wrong secret, changed body and old timestamp must fail verification. Duplicates and unknown types are accepted with 200, and simply not processed again or ignored. ? Why save real recorded payloads as fixtures? - Because the documentation's examples are always wrong - So the tests can make real payments end to end - Because they are already signed with the live secret + They show what the service really sends, not what the docs say A real payload may contain a field that is sometimes null or an extra field the examples never show. Remove personal data first. ? How does a service reach a webhook endpoint on your laptop during development? - Directly at http://127.0.0.1:8080 , its loopback address - Through the service's live-mode webhooks + Through a tunnel giving the local port a public HTTPS URL - By polling your laptop's port every minute The service cannot see your loopback address. A tunnel gives it a public HTTPS URL; use it in test mode and remove the subscription afterwards.

Making sure what you read applies to what you compile - version markers on reference pages, choosing the language standard with /std and -std, compiler support tables, __cplusplus and _MSVC_LANG, feature-test macros from the version header, deprecated and removed features, and reading the std::format specification as an example of a C++20 feature. Is it in my version? C++ gets a new standard every three years: C++11, 14, 17, 20, 23, 26. Reference pages mark what belongs to which: - (since C++17) next to an overload or member: only available from that version. - (until C++20) or (removed in C++20) : gone from that version on. - (deprecated in C++17) : still works, may be removed later; compilers warn. Before you rely on something, check the marker against the standard you compile with: Compiler Option Example --- --- --- MSVC /std:c++17 , /std:c++20 , /std:c++latest Visual Studio: Project Properties C/C++ Language GCC, Clang -std=c++17 , -std=c++20 , -std=c++2b Supported by my compiler? A feature being in the standard does not mean your compiler has it yet. cppreference's "Compiler support" pages list each feature and the first version of GCC, Clang and MSVC that implements it. std::format is C++20 but arrived in GCC 13 only; std::print is C++23 and needs recent compilers. Checking in code The macro __cplusplus holds the standard version ( 202002L for C++20). MSVC leaves it at 199711L unless you add /Zc:__cplusplus , and gives the real value in _MSVC_LANG . More precise are feature-test macros , defined by including <version : cpp include <iostream include <version int main() { ifdef __cpp_lib_format std::cout << "std::format available, version " << __cpp_lib_format << '
'; else std::cout << "no std::format
"; endif ifdef __cpp_lib_expected std::cout << "std::expected available
"; endif } Each library page lists its macro in a Notes table ("Feature-test macro: __cpp_lib_format , value 201907L"). Use them for code that must build on several compilers, with a fallback in the else . Reading a specification inside a page: std::format Some pages contain a small language of their own. The format specification for std::format (C++20) is one, and it rewards precise reading. A replacement field is {index:spec} , and the spec for numbers and strings is: text fill-and-align sign 0 width .precision L type Part Values Effect --- --- --- fill and align < left, right, ^ centre, optionally preceded by a fill character {: 6} pads with to width 6, right-aligned sign + always, - only negative (default), space: a space for positive {:+} gives +5 alternate form {: x} gives 0xff 0 pad numbers with zeros after the sign {:05} gives 00042 width minimum width {:8} precision digits after the point for floating point; maximum characters for strings {:.2f} , {:.3} type d x X b o for integers; f e g for floating point; s for strings {:b} gives binary Defaults matter, and the page states them: numbers align right, strings align left . So std::format(" {:6} ", 42) is 42 while std::format(" {:6} ", "ab") is ab . cpp include <format include <iostream int main() { std::cout << std::format(" {:6} {:6} {:^6} 
", 42, "ab", "mid"); std::cout << std::format("{: x} {:08.3f} {:+} {: <5} 
", 255, 3.14159, 7, "x"); } Check yourself quiz ? A member is marked (since C++20) and your project uses /std:c++17 . What happens if you call it? - It works, with a deprecation warning - It compiles but throws at run time + It does not compile, because it is not declared in C++17 mode - It calls the C++17 version of the member instead The standard library hides declarations that belong to later standards when you compile for an earlier one, so the name simply is not there. ? Why might __cplusplus say 199711L on MSVC even in C++20 mode? - MSVC does not support C++20 at all - 199711L is the code for C++20 + MSVC keeps the old value unless /Zc:__cplusplus is given - The macro is only set when compiling C code For compatibility, MSVC reports the 1997 value by default. _MSVC_LANG has the real one, or pass /Zc:__cplusplus to get the correct value. ? Where do you find a feature-test macro's name? - In the compiler's error message when the feature is missing + In the Notes section of the feature's reference page - In the first line of the header that declares the feature - It is always __cpp_ followed by the header name Each page names the macro, such as __cpp_lib_format , and the value that marks support. cppreference also has a full table of library feature-test macros. ? What does std::format(" {:6} ", "ab") produce? - ab , because text aligns right by default + ab , because strings align left by default - ab , because the width is ignored for strings - ab , because text is centred by default The format specification on the page says strings are left-aligned and numbers right-aligned unless you give < , or ^ .

Exchanging data with other programs - copying and pasting text through the clipboard, its formats and line endings, accepting files dropped from Explorer, file associations and the Recent list, and notifications in the taskbar. The clipboard The clipboard holds data in one or more formats at once: the same copied text can be offered as Unicode text, HTML and RTF, and the pasting program picks the richest one it understands. cpp-norun bool copyText(HWND owner, const std::wstring& text) { if (!OpenClipboard(owner)) return false; // another program may have it open: retry briefly EmptyClipboard(); // we become the owner const size_t bytes = (text.size() + 1) sizeof(wchar_t); HGLOBAL mem = GlobalAlloc(GMEM_MOVEABLE, bytes); if (mem) { memcpy(GlobalLock(mem), text.c_str(), bytes); GlobalUnlock(mem); if (!SetClipboardData(CF_UNICODETEXT, mem)) GlobalFree(mem); // on success the clipboard owns the memory } CloseClipboard(); return mem != nullptr; } std::wstring pasteText(HWND owner) { std::wstring text; if (!OpenClipboard(owner)) return text; if (HANDLE h = GetClipboardData(CF_UNICODETEXT)) { if (auto p = static_cast<const wchar_t (GlobalLock(h))) { text = p; GlobalUnlock(h); } } CloseClipboard(); return text; } Keep the clipboard open as briefly as possible: while one program has it open, nobody else can use it. Note who owns the memory: after a successful SetClipboardData , the clipboard does, and you must not free it; the data you get from GetClipboardData also belongs to the clipboard. Line endings Clipboard text uses CRLF ( 
 ) line endings by convention. A program that stores 
 internally converts to 
 when copying and back when pasting; a pasted text may also contain lone  or 
 from other programs, so normalise all three. The practice writes these conversions. Other formats - CF_HDROP : a list of files, as Explorer copies them (also used for drag and drop). - RegisterClipboardFormatW(L"HTML Format") : HTML with a small header giving offsets, which browsers and Office use. - Your own format, registered by name, to copy rich objects between instances of your program, plus CF_UNICODETEXT as a fallback for everyone else. To keep "Paste" enabled only when it makes sense, check IsClipboardFormatAvailable(CF_UNICODETEXT) in WM_INITMENUPOPUP (lesson 2.04). Dropping files The simplest way to accept files dragged from Explorer: cpp-norun DragAcceptFiles(hwnd, TRUE); // in WM_CREATE case WM_DROPFILES: { HDROP drop = reinterpret_cast<HDROP (wParam); const UINT count = DragQueryFileW(drop, 0xFFFFFFFF, nullptr, 0); for (UINT i = 0; i < count; ++i) { const UINT len = DragQueryFileW(drop, i, nullptr, 0); std::wstring path(len, L'\0'); DragQueryFileW(drop, i, path.data(), len + 1); openFile(path); } DragFinish(drop); return 0; } Inside, an HDROP is a DROPFILES header followed by the paths as a double-null-terminated list (each path null-terminated, an extra null at the end), the same layout as lesson 3.02's filters and REG_MULTI_SZ . Full OLE drag and drop ( RegisterDragDrop with an IDropTarget ) is needed to show a drop effect, accept text, or drag out of your program; it is more code but the same idea. File associations "Double-click a .xenonnote file to open it in my program" is a set of registry keys under HKCU\Software\Classes (lesson 5.02): a ProgID with the open command, and the extension pointing to it: HKCU\Software\Classes\.xenonnote (default) = Xenon.Note HKCU\Software\Classes\Xenon.Note\shell\open\command (default) = "C:\...\XenonNotes.exe" "%1" The "%1" (in quotes, for paths with spaces) is replaced by the file's path, which then arrives in your command line (lesson 5.03). After changing associations, call SHChangeNotify(SHCNE_ASSOCCHANGED, SHCNF_IDLIST, nullptr, nullptr) so Explorer notices. Installers usually write these keys. The Recent list SHAddToRecentDocs(SHARD_PATHW, path) adds a file to the user's recent documents, the taskbar jump list and the Start menu's recommendations, so the user can reopen it from outside your program. Notifications For "the search finished" while the window is in the background, flash the taskbar button ( FlashWindowEx with FLASHW_TRAY FLASHW_TIMERNOFG ) or show progress on it ( ITaskbarList3::SetProgressValue ). A notification-area icon ( Shell_NotifyIconW ) suits programs that run in the background. Check yourself quiz ? Who owns the memory passed to SetClipboardData after the call succeeds? + The clipboard; the program must not free or use it afterwards - The program, which must call GlobalFree after CloseClipboard - The program, until it calls EmptyClipboard again - Whichever program pastes it next On success the clipboard takes the memory. Only if SetClipboardData fails does the program free it with GlobalFree . ? Which line endings does clipboard text use? - LF ( 
 ), like most libraries - Whatever the copying program uses internally + CRLF ( 
 ), by convention - CR (  ) only Clipboard text uses CRLF by convention. A program that stores 
 converts when copying, and normalises lone  , lone 
 and 
 when pasting, since other programs may break the convention. ? How is the list of dropped files laid out inside an HDROP ? - An array of pointers to separately allocated path strings - One string with all the paths separated by semicolons - A count, then each path's length followed by its characters + A DROPFILES header, then null-terminated paths and one extra null It is a double-null-terminated list, the same layout as the file dialog filters and REG_MULTI_SZ . DragQueryFileW reads it for you.
